# Core conventions

## Code style

- No `console.log` debug dumps or commented-out code
- Avoid eslint-disable comments; only when no reasonable alternative exists (document why)
- No default exports — use named exports
  - **Exception:** Next.js `app/**/page.tsx`, `app/**/layout.tsx`, and config files (`next.config.ts`, `drizzle.config.ts`, etc.) require default exports
- No long ternaries; don't name your own functions `handle*` — name by what the function does
  - **Exception:** library APIs like `form.handleSubmit` (react-hook-form) or DOM/React event props
- File naming: hooks → camelCase, components → PascalCase, other → kebab-case
- One param per function, or an object param
- Prefer `args.field` over destructuring `args` in long functions (short functions excepted)
- Prefer colocated code; no barrel `index.ts` re-exports
- No verbose JSDoc (@param, @returns, etc.)
- No explicit return types on functions
- No px values in custom CSS — use rem (Tailwind arbitrary values like `shadow-[0_8px_24px]` and canvas drawing are fine)
- No inline style overrides on UI kit components — use variants/components
- Booleans: prefix with `is`, `has`, `should`, `does`
- JSX conditionals: use `condition && <Node />` for optional / branch UI — never `condition ? <Node /> : null`
  ```tsx
  // Preferred
  {item.notes && <p>{item.notes}</p>}
  {!hasItems && <EmptyState />}
  {hasItems && <ItemList items={items} />}

  // Avoid
  {item.notes ? <p>{item.notes}</p> : null}
  {hasItems ? <ItemList items={items} /> : <EmptyState />}
  ```
- Omit curly braces when the body is a single statement — e.g. early `return`, single-line `if`, single-expression arrow functions:
  ```typescript
  // Preferred
  if (loadErr) return err(loadErr);
  if (!data) return null;
  const double = (n: number) => n * 2;

  // Avoid
  if (loadErr) {
    return err(loadErr);
  }
  ```
  Use braces when the block has multiple statements or when nesting `if`/`else` branches.

## Types

- Prefer value-optional (`field: T | undefined`) over key-optional (`field?: T`)
- Convert `null` → `undefined` on server before sending to clients
- Exception: UI props like `className?: string` may use key-optional

## Result pattern

- Use `tryCatch` / `tryCatchDb` + `ok` / `err` from `@/lib/result`
- Always destructure: `const [error, data] = ...` — never index (`result[0]`)

## Database (Drizzle)

- All `pgTable` declarations use `...timestamps` from `@/lib/db/timestamps` (`createdAt`, `updatedAt`, `deletedAt`)
- `updated_at` is maintained by DB trigger `trg_set_updated_at` → `public.set_updated_at()`. Do not rely on Drizzle `.$onUpdate`. New tables that spread `...timestamps` must attach the same trigger in their migration:

  ```sql
  CREATE TRIGGER trg_set_updated_at
  BEFORE UPDATE ON public.<table>
  FOR EACH ROW
  EXECUTE FUNCTION public.set_updated_at();
  ```
- Soft delete is mandatory for content rows. Use helpers from `@/lib/db/not-deleted` — do not write `isNull(table.deletedAt)` or set `deletedAt` by hand:
  - Reads / updates / “does this row exist?”: `.where(and(..., notDeleted(table)))`
  - Joins to content tables: also `notDeleted(joinedTable)` so a soft-deleted parent cannot leak
  - Deletes: `.update(table).set(softDeleteNow()).where(and(eq(table.id, id), notDeleted(table)))`
  - Never `.delete()` content tables
- Exception — composite-PK junction tables may stay **hard-delete** (re-inserting the same pair would conflict with a soft-deleted row)
- Unique slugs use a **partial** unique index `WHERE deleted_at IS NULL` so a slug can be reused after soft-delete. Do not put `.unique()` on the slug column:

  ```typescript
  uniqueIndex('items_slug_active_unique')
    .on(table.slug)
    .where(sql`${table.deletedAt} is null`)
  ```
- Naming: `table_*`, `query_*`, `view_*`, `pgEnum_*`
- SQL table names: existing tables use plural (`users`, `items`); keep consistency within the project
- Zod schemas: `*Schema` suffix
- No single-letter SQL aliases

### RLS (Row Level Security)

This project uses **two separate layers**:

1. **App tables** (`users`, `items`, etc.) — accessed only via oRPC + Drizzle with `DATABASE_URL` on the server. Auth/authorization lives in `procedure_protected` handlers, not Postgres RLS. Table RLS is **not required** today.
2. **Supabase Storage** — clients upload directly, so RLS policies on `storage.objects` are defined in `integrations/supabase/policies/*.sql`.

`.enableRLS()` in Drizzle only runs `ALTER TABLE ... ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` — it **turns on** the RLS switch for a table. It does **not** create policies. Policies (`CREATE POLICY ...`) are separate SQL that define who can read/write rows.

```
enableRLS()  →  "RLS is active on this table"
CREATE POLICY → "authenticated users can SELECT their own rows"
```

Both are needed for RLS to actually work. Without `enableRLS()`, policies have no effect. Without policies, RLS-enabled tables deny everyone (except superuser/service_role).

Do **not** add `.enableRLS()` to app tables unless you also add matching policies and plan to query via Supabase client roles (`anon`/`authenticated`). Our server uses `DATABASE_URL` which typically bypasses RLS anyway.

## UI language

- All user-facing strings in **English** (buttons, errors, toasts, dialogs)
- Server `message` fields: user-friendly English
- No i18n libraries unless explicitly requested

## TanStack Query in components

- Check `.data` first, then errors, then loading — not `isSuccess`
- Use `usePublicQuery` / `useSessionQuery` — not raw `useQuery`

## Skip commenting on

- Auto-generated migrations
- `'use client'`, `'use server'`, `server-only`, `client-only` directives
